{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790186999,"nonce":"PPCiscJu91kv8sTBJzZwkMIz2T8=","title":"Decided 2026-09-23 with Luis, in one sitting.","message":"1. Built-ins are three: title (text), type (type id), archived (bool). Status is NOT built in: it is an ordinary preset field of kind enum-with-category that presets put on every work type. Engine behaviour keyed on categories (hide completed/canceled, status:open shorthand, weekly report, Jira resolution mapping) applies where a type has such a field and degrades to \"never done\" where it does not; archived is the guaranteed default-listing filter. A type with no completed category is fine.\n\n2. No field roles. Consumers bind fields by key in their own config: the Gantt flow says start: start_date, end: due; the planning flow says estimate: story_points; my-tasks says assignee: assignee. Presets ship their flows with bindings filled in. The closed role set leaves the engine; \"found by kind and role\" is replaced by \"flows bind by key\".\n\n3. on_open / on_close are dropped from the schema. A close verb, if any, is a flow with a configured target value; `git work issue set ID status done` always works.\n\n4. The config entity is a plain document: kind, key, a map of JSON attributes with last-writer-wins per attribute, archived. Four operations: Create{kind,key}, Set{name,value}, Remove{name}, SetArchived{bool} (+ dag NoOp/SetMetadata). The item map is folded into attributes (enum values live at attribute names `values/\u003cid\u003e`; per-attribute merge is exactly what per-item merge was). Identity is (kind, key) for every kind; a field's key is `\u003ctype\u003e/\u003ckey\u003e` by convention, so `type` is not a member of Create.\n\n5. Kinds are three: type (key `\u003ctype\u003e`), field (key `\u003ctype\u003e/\u003cfield\u003e`), flow (key `\u003cflow\u003e`). Relation is folded into field: kinds relation / multi-relation carry inverse and target_types as attributes, cardinality is the kind. View is not a kind for now: a flow is a selection (jq program) plus what to do with it, and rendering as a list or a board is an action; add `view` later only if a live view needs something a flow cannot express. Namespaces: refs/work-schema (type, field) and refs/work-flows (flow); refs/work-views is dropped.","files":null}]}